@@s001 Weighted interval scheduling: DP on the intervals
@@s002 Jobs with a start, an end and a <b>value</b>. Now the values are different: the greedy by end is not enough. The DP is needed.
@@s003 I sort by end: j1(1–3, v2), j2(2–5, v4), j3(4–7, v4), j4(6–9, v7), j5(8–10, v2). For each job <b>p(j)</b> = last job compatible with j.
@@s004 <code>OPT(j) = max( v_j + OPT(p(j)) , OPT(j−1) )</code>: I take j and the best of the compatible ones before, or I skip it. OPT(1) = 2, OPT(2) = 4.
@@s005 OPT(3) = max( 4 + OPT(1), OPT(2) ) = max(6, 4) = 6. OPT(4) = max( 7 + OPT(2), OPT(3) ) = max(11, 6) = 11.
@@s006 OPT(5) = max( 2 + OPT(3), OPT(4) ) = max(8, 11) = 11. Answer: <b>11</b>, with j2, j4 (4 + 7).
@@s007 ok:taken,no:skipped,ora:job under examination
@@s008 Weighted interval scheduling: DP on the intervals
@@s009 When the jobs have different values, the greedy by end is not enough. One uses the DP on the intervals sorted by end: for each job, the best between taking it and skipping it.
@@s010 The recurrence
@@s011 Sort the jobs by end. For each job <code>j</code>, <code>p(j)</code> is the last job, in the order, compatible with <code>j</code> (it ends before its start). The recurrence: <code>OPT(j) = max( v_j + OPT(p(j)), OPT(j − 1) )</code>, with <code>OPT(0) = 0</code>.
@@s012 Take or skip, job by job
@@s013 Cost and reconstruction
@@s014 Sorting costs <code>O(n log n)</code>, <code>p(j)</code> is found with a binary search, then each <code>OPT(j)</code> is a comparison: total <code>O(n log n)</code>. For the solution, go back: if <code>v_j + OPT(p(j)) ≥ OPT(j − 1)</code> the job <code>j</code> is taken, and you move to <code>p(j)</code>; otherwise to <code>j − 1</code>.
@@s015 The code, side by side
@@s016 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
